\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Paired AUC of the differencing attacker against $m$, pooled over the three attackers (120, 120, 80 and 40 probes at $m = 1$, 10, 100 and 1,000). Above the dashed line the observable moves as predicted for $W_1$ (latency up; count, recall and score down), below it the other way. Gray bands: the range of paired AUCs between builds of identical content, for FAISS's post-filter (first panel) and pgvector's exact search (fifth). Pooled curves mix attackers, so FAISS's first-panel fall at $m = 1{,}000$ comes from the attacker reading 1\%. Keyword panel: the worlds differ in all 1,233 secrets at every $m$; open squares are \texttt  {ts\_rank\_cd}'s score, filled squares its latency. Table~\ref {tab:side-auc} in Appendix~\ref {app:side-results} has the values.}}{17}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:side-auc}{{3}{17}{Paired AUC of the differencing attacker against $m$, pooled over the three attackers (120, 120, 80 and 40 probes at $m = 1$, 10, 100 and 1,000). Above the dashed line the observable moves as predicted for $W_1$ (latency up; count, recall and score down), below it the other way. Gray bands: the range of paired AUCs between builds of identical content, for FAISS's post-filter (first panel) and pgvector's exact search (fifth). Pooled curves mix attackers, so FAISS's first-panel fall at $m = 1{,}000$ comes from the attacker reading 1\%. Keyword panel: the worlds differ in all 1,233 secrets at every $m$; open squares are \texttt {ts\_rank\_cd}'s score, filled squares its latency. Table~\ref {tab:side-auc} in Appendix~\ref {app:side-results} has the values}{figure.caption.24}{}}
